{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013Wcak2je4tvaC7AumS6CU9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. They derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. Key components:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n**Current State**: \n- All PRDs are written (prd001-prd010)\n- All use cases are written\n- All test suites are written\n- NO code has been implemented yet\n- Current release: rel01.0 (in_progress) - ts specification and differential test\n\n**Roadmap**:\n- rel01.0 (in_progress): ts - use case rel01.0-uc001-ts (in_progress)\n- rel01.1 (pending): wc, cat, sponge\n- rel02.0 (pending)\n- rel02.1 (pending): ls extended\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel01.0 with ts in progress. To get ts working, I need to build pkg/testutils first since it provides the differential testing harness that cmd/ts depends on. Following the dependency ordering, libraries come before their consumers, so pkg/testutils is the critical path blocker.\n\nLooking at what prd001-testutils requires, it's a substantial implementation covering test data structures, binary execution with timeouts, output comparison and reporting, composable normalizers, and file-state tracking. Given the 250-350 line constraint for a single task, I need to scope this carefully to deliver the core foundation without trying to fit everything at once.\n\nFor rel01.0 focused on the `ts` tool, I can defer file-state comparison since that's needed for `sponge` in rel01.1. The essential pieces are the DiffTest struct, binary execution logic, output comparison, and normalization hooks—particularly the TimestampNormalizer that `ts` will depend on. That combination should fit within the line budget while providing a solid foundation for the test utilities.\n\nNow I'm mapping this against the PRD requirements to anchor the task properly. The implementation covers R1 through R4 (DiffTest struct, binary execution, comparison and reporting, and normalization), which breaks down into about 15 specific sub-requirements across those four areas. I'm mapping out the requirement groups more systematically now. R1 covers the DiffTest struct with its fields and normalization function, R2 handles executing both binaries with matching inputs and configurable timeouts, R3 manages the comparison logic with normalization applied and detailed divergence reporting through subtests, and R4 defines the normalizer interface and implementations. That gives me four groups, but I need to reach the 5-8 range, so I should break these down further—maybe separating the struct definition from the normalization concerns, or splitting execution from output capture. I'm settling on 8 requirements that map cleanly to the PRD sections. Now I need to check the acceptance criteria—the PRD specifies AC1 through AC6, and I can see AC1 requires RunDiffTests to be callable directly from test functions without additional setup, while AC2 is about passing tests.\n\nLooking at the rest of the criteria, AC3 covers the TimestampNormalizer behavior, AC5 validates timeout handling with clear error messages, and AC6 ensures the code compiles cleanly under golangci-lint. AC4 about file-state comparison is deferred, so that leaves me with 5 relevant acceptance criteria for this task.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the scope—the decomposition guidance says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, so this task should focus on the implementation itself being correct and compilable, not on test execution outcomes.\n\nSince I'm creating just one task here, I should combine the types and implementation together given their tight coupling. Tests will come later as a separate task.\n\nFor the file naming, the Go package convention requires naming the primary source file after the main exported type rather than the package name, so this should be `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` with `DiffTest` as the primary type.\n\nLooking at design decisions to include, I need 3-5 for a code task. The DiffTest struct serves as the primary type, and the file naming follows the convention of using the type name rather than the package name. I'm checking my work against the PRD to make sure I'm deriving design decisions rather than inventing them. Looking at the concrete requirements: the DiffTest struct, the Normalize function type signature, the 10-second default timeout, running subtests with t.Run, accepting a slice of normalizers applied in order, and the RunDiffTests API signature from the architecture. These are all explicitly specified, so I should stick to what's actually required rather than adding my own assumptions.\n\nNow I need to write the task description. The title should reference the requirement IDs per the naming convention, so something like \"pkg/testutils: Implement DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests (prd001 R1-R4)\" captures the scope. Before I write the full YAML, I should estimate whether this fits as a single task or if it's too large to complete in one go.\n\nLooking at the components I need to implement, the DiffTest struct itself is fairly compact, but RunDiffTests is the heavy lifting with around 80 lines for the main loop and subtest execution, plus helper functions for executing binaries and comparing outputs. The normalizer logic adds another 50-60 lines across the different normalizer implementations. All told, I'm looking at roughly 250 lines of code, which sits comfortably in the expected range for a task of this complexity.\n\nFor the required reading, the stitch agent will need access to the PRD for testutils to understand the requirements, the architecture documentation to see how this component fits into the larger system, and the go.mod file to confirm the module name and any dependencies.\n\nNow I need to map out the actual file paths and structure. Since we're building from scratch with no existing code, I'll organize this around the primary exported type—DiffTest—which means the main file should be difftest.go. The PRD specifies the key pieces: the DiffTest struct itself, the Normalize function type, RunDiffTests as the orchestrator, and TimestampNormalizer as a concrete implementation. I'll need to decide whether to keep everything in one file or split the normalizers into a separate file, but given the scope, keeping it consolidated makes sense. I'm settling on putting everything in `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` to keep it simple, then I need to make sure the stitch agent has access to the right documentation—the PRD, architecture file, go.mod, and test suite references.\n\nLooking at the dependency chain, implementing `pkg/testutils` first makes the most sense since everything else depends on it, which aligns with the principles of building libraries before their consumers.\n\nNow I need to verify this task actually delivers what's needed for rel01.0—specifically R1 through R4, not R5 which is deferred for the sponge work in rel01.1. Since I'm targeting 5-8 requirements and have expanded the four top-level ones into eight specific requirements, that fits the constraint. For acceptance criteria, I should focus on the core deliverables: the DiffTest struct with all its fields matching the spec, and the RunDiffTests function signature.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, I'm mapping each one directly to the requirements—AC1 covers the struct definition, AC2 the function signature, AC3-AC4 handle binary execution and timeout behavior, AC5-AC6 address the comparison and reporting logic, AC7 specifies the timestamp normalization, and AC8 ensures code quality. That gives me eight criteria, which aligns with the 5-8 requirement target.\n\nActually, I can tighten this. Let me consolidate the overlapping criteria—merging the struct definition with the function signature, combining execution and timeout into one criterion, and folding the divergence report details into the comparison logic. That brings me down to seven criteria, which fits the range better and removes redundancy while keeping all the essential requirements covered.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the output. Let me think through the file structure—I need `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` as the main implementation file. Since this is a new package within the existing module, I don't need to modify go.mod, and the Write tool will handle directory creation. No package documentation file is necessary here.\n\nDefining the DiffTest struct with its fields: Args for command arguments, Stdin for input (nil means no stdin), Env for environment variables (nil means inherit), ExitCode for the expected exit code, and Normalize as a slice of normalization functions. The Normalize type itself is a function signature that takes and returns a byte slice.\n\nNow I need to implement RunDiffTests, which takes a testing context, paths to both the Go and reference binaries, and a slice of test cases, then runs each one as a named subtest. For each test, I'll execute both binaries with identical arguments, stdin, and environment, capturing their stdout, stderr, and exit codes separately. Each invocation gets a configurable timeout with a 10-second default, and the test fails if the timeout is exceeded.\n\nBefore comparing outputs, I apply any normalization functions from the DiffTest in sequence to both binaries' stdout and stderr, then do a byte-for-byte comparison of the normalized outputs and exact comparison of exit codes. If anything diverges, I report the test arguments, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), both versions of stdout and stderr, and both exit codes.\n\nI'm also implementing a TimestampNormalizer that handles common strftime patterns...\n\nDefining the normalizer composition function... Looking at the acceptance criteria for the test harness, I need to ensure RunDiffTests can be called directly from test functions, that binary execution properly isolates stdout, stderr, and exit codes with timeout handling, that normalization is applied before comparison, and that each test runs as a named subtest for clear failure reporting.\n\nNow I'm noticing a tension in the requirements—the PRD mentions an optional normalization function in one place but then describes accepting a slice of normalizers applied in sequence elsewhere. Let me trace through what the actual implementation needs to support.\n\nLooking at the requirements more carefully, R4.3 is explicit that the Normalize field must be a slice of functions applied in order, which overrides the singular wording in R1.1. That's clear.\n\nBut I also need to verify whether DiffTest requires a Name field. R3.6 mentions running each test as a named subtest with t.Run, which strongly suggests the struct needs a Name field to identify individual test failures, even though R1.1 doesn't explicitly list it.\n\nI'm also realizing I haven't fully covered R2.4 and R2.5 yet—those requirements about suppressing stdout/stderr during passing tests and supporting a working directory parameter. Let me make sure those are incorporated into my requirements breakdown. I've mapped out 8 requirements covering the core functionality, and now I'm settling on 5 key design decisions: the primary source file structure, the entry point signature, timeout handling with a 10-second default, how normalizers chain together in order, and using a temporary directory as the default working location. For acceptance criteria, I'm aiming for 5-8 items starting with defining the DiffTest struct and Normalize type. I've trimmed the acceptance criteria down to 7 items by consolidating and removing redundancy. Now I'm moving on to write out the final YAML specification with precise formatting.\n\nActually, I need to refine R1 to include the `Name` field for test naming and the `WorkDir` field for directory control—both are important struct fields that should be documented alongside the other parameters.\n\nLet me reconsider the exit code field. The DiffTest struct has an expected exit code, but the comparison logic compares the two binaries' exit codes against each other, not against a stored value. I'm checking the acceptance criteria to clarify whether the struct field is actually needed or if I'm misinterpreting the requirements. the comparison logic compares the two binary exit codes, so the expected exit code field might serve as an additional assertion or fallback when the reference binary isn't available. I'll include it as the PRD specifies and move forward. I also need to decide whether to add a Name field to DiffTest—while not explicitly mentioned in R1.1, it's implied by R3.6 since t.Run requires a test name, and having an explicit field is cleaner than deriving it from the args or index.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71816,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71816},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"677f4d86-1cfb-4f53-af73-d135aa0240e0","uuid":"7a60bf70-0bd2-4d9d-bae6-d10549986425"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013Wcak2je4tvaC7AumS6CU9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the project context, I'll analyze the state and produce the task.\n\n**Project State Summary:**\n- go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development: derive specs from C source, generate Go implementations via AI, verify via differential testing against GNU reference binaries\n- All PRDs, use cases, and test suites are written (prd001-prd010, 4 test suites, 7 use cases)\n- No implementation code exists yet\n- Current release: rel01.0 (in_progress) -- ts utility, use case rel01.0-uc001-ts\n\n**Priority Analysis:**\n- rel01.0 needs: pkg/testutils (harness) and cmd/ts (utility)\n- ts depends on pkg/testutils for RunDiffTests and TimestampNormalizer\n- Per canonical ordering: libraries before consumers → pkg/testutils first\n- Per P2: implementation and tests are separate tasks\n- R5 (file-state comparison) is needed for sponge in rel01.1, can be deferred\n\nThe single most valuable task is implementing the core pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R4), which unblocks all utility development."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71816,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71816},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"677f4d86-1cfb-4f53-af73-d135aa0240e0","uuid":"a3da884a-6a6d-4612-b54d-2fbac8a0a85d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013Wcak2je4tvaC7AumS6CU9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016RPBShyhLLm1dVGwo8QLXA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/petardjukic/2025-objectives/go-unix-utils/.cobbler/measure-20260224-223516.yaml","content":"- index: 0\n  title: \"pkg/testutils: Implement DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests (prd001 R1-R4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (full PRD for pkg/testutils -- DiffTest struct, binary execution, comparison, normalization)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component description and role in the pipeline)\n      - go.mod (module path for package imports)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, Normalize type, RunDiffTests entry point, binary execution, comparison logic, TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define DiffTest struct with fields: Name (string, used as subtest name per prd001-testutils R3.6), Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte, nil means binary receives no stdin per prd001-testutils R1.2), Env ([]string, nil means inherit test process environment per prd001-testutils R1.3), ExitCode (int, expected exit code per prd001-testutils R1.1), WorkDir (string, empty means use per-test temp directory per prd001-testutils R2.5), and Normalize ([]Normalize, nil or empty means no normalization per prd001-testutils R4.3). Define Normalize as func([]byte) []byte per prd001-testutils R1.4.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that iterates the test slice and runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run using DiffTest.Name. Per prd001-testutils R2.1, R3.6, and AC1.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"For each DiffTest, execute both goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin, and Env. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently. When WorkDir is empty, run both binaries in a per-test temporary directory. Must not write any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run. Per prd001-testutils R2.1-R2.2, R2.4-R2.5.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation using context.WithTimeout. The default timeout must be 10 seconds. The test fails with a clear timeout message if either binary exceeds the timeout. Per prd001-testutils R2.3.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Apply the DiffTest.Normalize functions in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison. Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte, compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte, and compare exit codes exactly. Any difference must be reported as a test failure. Per prd001-testutils R3.1-R3.4.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"On divergence, report in the failure message: the DiffTest Args, the Stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, the reference binary stderr, the Go binary stderr, and both exit codes. Per prd001-testutils R3.5.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement TimestampNormalizer as a Normalize function that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with the fixed placeholder string 'TIMESTAMP'. Must handle patterns matching the default ts format '%b %d %H:%M:%S' (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') and HH:MM:SS elapsed time formats. Per prd001-testutils R4.2 and AC3.\"\n      - id: R8\n        text: \"Implement ComposeNormalizers(normalizers ...Normalize) Normalize that returns a single Normalize function applying the given normalizers in order. When called with no arguments, returns nil. Per prd001-testutils R4.1 and R4.3.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Primary exported type is DiffTest; primary source file is difftest.go (named after primary exported type, not package name, per project naming convention).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Entry point signature is RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) per prd001-testutils AC1.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Timeout is implemented via context.WithTimeout wrapping exec.CommandContext; default is 10 seconds per prd001-testutils R2.3.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"DiffTest.Normalize is []Normalize (a slice applied in order) per prd001-testutils R4.3. Nil or empty slice means no normalization is applied.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"When DiffTest.WorkDir is empty, both binaries run in a per-test temporary directory created via t.TempDir() per prd001-testutils R2.5.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest struct and Normalize function type are defined with all fields specified in prd001-testutils R1.1-R1.4.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) is callable from a TestXxx function with no other setup per prd001-testutils AC1.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Binary execution captures stdout, stderr, and exit code independently. No output is written to the test process during a passing run per prd001-testutils R2.4.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Timeout defaults to 10 seconds. A DiffTest exceeding the timeout fails with a clear timeout message per prd001-testutils R2.3 and AC5.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Comparison applies normalization then diffs byte-for-byte. Divergence report includes args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), both stdout, both stderr, and both exit codes per prd001-testutils R3.1-R3.5.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps with 'TIMESTAMP' placeholder. ComposeNormalizers chains multiple normalizers in order per prd001-testutils R4.2-R4.3 and AC3.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint per prd001-testutils AC6.\"\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71816,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71816},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"677f4d86-1cfb-4f53-af73-d135aa0240e0","uuid":"ea7e259d-7f4b-42a8-b1de-207ded590146"}
